plan React
title   React ownership, attachment and passive views
status  proposed
goal
    Components read consistent data and keep stable actor handles without starting work in render.
outcomes
    O1  Provider and Runtime ownership
        One FlowProvider gives a React tree one Runtime, constructed and owned by the Provider or by the host, and
        renders children without suspending.

        flow-state/react exposes <FlowProvider setup={setup}> and <FlowProvider runtime={runtime}>.
        With setup, the Provider constructs and owns one Runtime per setup scope: a new setup value
        with the same scope keeps the existing Runtime, while a changed scope (sign-in, sign-out,
        account switch) constructs a fresh Runtime and disposes the old one once it has been unused
        for longer than the 100 ms grace, so Strict Mode remounts keep it.

        With runtime, the host owns construction and disposal and the Provider never disposes.
        Either form renders children immediately without suspending or showing a readiness fallback.
        Its first effect calls ready(), so a browser app restores without a separate host call;
        unmount cancels only that observer, not shared readiness.

        Readiness failure reaches a React error boundary on the next render. Hooks use the nearest
        Provider; unrelated nested Runtimes never share data or ownership. `useView` rejects a
        foreign Runtime's handle with ForeignActorRef before subscribing.

        An old scope's Runtime is never left running. Isolation follows @plan.Runtime.O7. All
        `useView` reads in one render see one consistent version across actors and store entries.
    O2  Hooks and the first render
        Hooks return stable actor handles whose first render is pure and starts no work.

        useActor(machine, options?) returns a local actor handle for the component incarnation:
        fresh memory and state, with the write lanes of its local identity (machine, input by
        Equal.equals) adopted, so a remounted or revealed local actor with the same input receives
        its predecessor's lanes. useActorByRef(ref, options) ensures a shared actor and returns a
        handle for a non-null ref; any options object, including {}, selects ensure.

        A value-named ref takes no input: useActorByRef(ref, {}) ensures it, and the ref's form is
        fixed per machine in the types. useActorByRef(ref) only looks up and returns Actor | null: a
        lookup of an identity with a record returns the record view, never null; with neither a live
        actor nor a record it returns null.

        A null ref returns null and starts nothing. Invalid, foreign, opaque-local or mismatched
        refs reject with ForeignActorRef. Lookup neither subscribes nor holds a use.

        Before attachment, ensure returns a stable handle with a pure snapshot: default states, the
        memory built from the Flow.input fields, constant Flow.initial values and computed initials,
        available passive provider context, active lifecycle, no issues, active resource lanes
        Pending with undefined data or a pure placeholder; inactive lanes absent.

        A pre-attach handle of an identity the Runtime holds a record for reads the Runtime's copy,
        so a quick return never flashes the initial state. Pending lanes alone do not mark
        optimistic writes predicted. Attached actors show committed data. Render registers nothing,
        subscribes to nothing, starts no work, writes no storage and emits no evidence, including
        lazy adoption.

        Creation must be pure: a default is pure, and creation may run in discarded renders and on
        both server and client, so it reads no clock or randomness; each field has one default, the
        same at creation and restore, evaluated per actor. Fresh attachment keeps the prepared
        memory; restored actor precedence is @plan.Persistence.O4. Server and hydration snapshots
        follow @plan.ServerRendering.O4.

        Handles, sends and typed event constructors stay stable for the same identity. Equivalent
        refs resolve the same actor; changing the ref resolves the new identity. Local input is read
        once; changing local machine or input requires a keyed remount and emits a development
        diagnostic. A ref never resets an existing actor; conflicting refs follow @plan.Runtime.O3.
    O3  Attach, detach, Strict Mode and grace
        After commit and readiness, an ensuring hook attaches the same handle through the Runtime.
        It uses current provider context without replaying creation, input or `onContext` work. Lazy
        admission follows @plan.LazyFeatures.O2; failed admission leaves no partial actor.

        An ensuring hook is a use of its actor from its passive commit effect to that effect's
        cleanup, including while its attach waits for readiness, lazy adoption, a Hydrate boundary
        or a disposing predecessor; re-suspension keeps it, and React Activity hiding ends it.
        Before attachment, at most 64 sends buffer in FIFO order; send 65 throws
        FlowError(AdmissionOverloaded) without accepting the event.

        Attachment delivers accepted sends exactly once through @plan.Runtime.O4, after a new
        incarnation's lane deliveries. Sends through a handle follow the Runtime's send table
        (@plan.Runtime.O4): a send never creates and may restore a persisted actor from its record;
        discarded renders never deliver their buffered events to another component incarnation.

        A snapshots subscription completes when its subscriber stops or after the terminal snapshot
        of `disposeActor`, forget or Runtime disposal. Subscribers hear one notify per task: the
        first slice end in a task notifies, and later ones in that task coalesce into one in the
        next task.

        Local cleanup leaves the actor active for the grace, 100 milliseconds on the Runtime Clock.
        A local actor remounted within its grace reuses that actor and cancels its end; expiry ends
        it through the Runtime, except that work in flight keeps it until that work's ending's turn
        has run. During the grace placements run and schedules wait.

        Strict Mode cleanup/setup never duplicates work or loses the actor. Shared ensuring hooks
        count as uses; lifetime follows @plan.Runtime.O3. Dropping a JavaScript handle disposes
        nothing. Explicit disposal is visible to all users; a mounted ensuring hook re-ensures a
        fresh incarnation under @plan.Runtime.O3. Unmount while awaiting readiness never admits
        later.

        Reattach during disposal waits for cleanup; failed cleanup surfaces through @O5. React
        Activity hiding ends the uses inside the hidden subtree; a local actor hidden beyond its
        grace ends, and reveal creates a fresh one (adopting its identity's lanes). To keep state
        across hiding, persist the machine or hold a use outside the hidden subtree. Selectors read
        operations.
    O4  Eligibility in views
        A view reads an actor's snapshot through a selector with the selector's exact return type, and reads time
        through the Runtime Clock.

        useView(actor, select) accepts an exact handle and preserves the selector's exact return
        type. Nullable lookup must be branched first; Machines, refs and comparator arguments are
        rejected. useNow(every) returns the Runtime Clock's epoch milliseconds and re-renders at
        that Duration; views read time through it rather than Date.now, so Stories and StoryFrame
        control it.

        Selectors read frozen state, memory (Flow.private fields omitted from the view types),
        context, issues, lifecycle, schedules and operations: s.operations.<name>, O.x.get(s, k), a
        fragment's write lanes through Fragment.O.x.get(s, k) and its rows through
        s.operations.<instance>.<name>.

        A disposed snapshot keeps its last state, memory and context with lifecycle disposed. State
        tokens narrow snapshot types; check(s, event) follows @plan.Machines.O4. Rows follow
        @plan.Operations.O6. contract.get (proposed name) and Entity.get are passive: reading never
        fetches, mutates or retains data.

        Selectors start no work and acquire no ownership. Changes to currently selected keys update
        the view even without an actor change; conditional reads replace those dependencies.
        Selected values compare with Equal.equals, and unchanged lanes keep identity. Changing
        selector identity does not resubscribe to the actor.
    O5  Errors in views
        A selector exception reaches the nearest React error boundary. Reading unchanged relevant
        data rethrows the same exception without reevaluation; a later matching publication retries,
        while unrelated updates do not. An attached view failure leaves committed data and siblings
        intact.

        Initial attachment or hydration validation failure aborts admission without a partial actor.
        Readiness, admission, lazy adoption, provenance and cleanup failures retain the FlowError
        classification of @plan.Diagnostics.O1 and rethrow on the next render.

        Boundary reset allows adoption retry on a later attachment, never by loading during render.
        Resource failure lanes and typed machine errors remain view data; they do not automatically
        throw. Unknown commit reconciliation follows @plan.Operations.O3. Hooks never suspend.
verification
    V1  Type/mount tests prove exact hook types, Provider isolation, readiness and boundary errors.
    V2  Render tests prove pure snapshots, stable handles, ref changes and no discarded-render
        work.
    V3  Attach tests prove 64 buffered sends, FIFO delivery once after lane deliveries, current
        context and unmount safety.
    V4  Lifecycle tests prove the grace, Strict Mode, uses and work in flight, re-ensure, local
        remount within the grace and cleanup failures.
    V5  View tests prove consistent passive reads, key updates, equality and isolated error
        retries.
    V6  Hydration tests prove matching first HTML and no duplicated work.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        The private attachment and subscription implementation; how selector failures are cached
        for unchanged relevant data; development diagnostic wording for local machine/input
        changes; lifecycle race generation and browser test coverage.
